.TH SISTA-PAWN 3 "2026-10-04" "Sista 3.0.3"
.SH NAME
sista-pawn \- represent a styled object in a field
.SH LIBRARY
Sista C++ library (libSista, \fI-lSista\fP)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <sista/pawn.hpp>

.B "sista::Pawn::Pawn(char symbol, const Coordinates &position,"
.B "                  const ANSISettings &settings);"
.B "void sista::Pawn::setCoordinates(const Coordinates &position);"
.B "Coordinates sista::Pawn::getCoordinates() const;"
.B "void sista::Pawn::setSettings(const ANSISettings &settings);"
.B "ANSISettings sista::Pawn::getSettings() const;"
.B "void sista::Pawn::setSymbol(char symbol);"
.B "char sista::Pawn::getSymbol() const;"
.B "virtual void sista::Pawn::print() const;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Pawn
combines a character, coordinates, and ANSI settings.
It is designed to be subclassed for application-specific objects.
.PP
Movement is managed by
.BR Field .
Changing a pawn's coordinates or symbol directly does not update or
redraw an associated field.
.SH RETURN VALUE
Getters return the stored property.
Setters and
.B print()
return no value.
.SH CAVEATS
Fields normally retain pawns through
.BR std::shared_ptr<Pawn> .
Do not give a field a non-owning pointer disguised as a shared owner.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
auto pawn = std::make_shared<sista::Pawn>(
    '@', sista::Coordinates(2, 3), sista::ANSISettings());
field.addPawn(pawn);
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sista-ansi (3),
.BR sista-coordinates (3),
.BR sista-field (3),
.BR sista (7)
